<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1127905" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071113/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-1.png" alt width="1138" height="1600" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071113/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-1.png 1138w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071113/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-1-256x360.png 256w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071113/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-1-728x1024.png 728w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071113/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-1-171x240.png 171w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071113/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-1-768x1080.png 768w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071113/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-1-1092x1536.png 1092w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1138px) 100vw, 1138px"></p>
<p>For generations of kids in the twentieth century, few pleasures were as intoxicating as a trip to the comic-book shop with the week’s allowance fresh in hand. Those of us who grew up in the nineties had any number of high-production-value paper superhero spectacles from major publishers on which to blow our money. Personally, I preferred the greater value of <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2024/02/the-disney-artist-who-developed-donald-duck-remained-anonymous-for-years.html">Carl Barks</a>’ old Uncle Scrooge and Donald Duck stories then being reprinted. But nothing offered quite so much value as the cardboard boxes, kept below the main shelves, filled as they were with previously owned comic books from the fifties, sixties, seventies, and eighties. As I recall, a couple of dollars could get you a whole afternoon’s reading material.</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1127903" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071049/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-4.png" alt width="1104" height="1600" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071049/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-4.png 1104w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071049/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-4-248x360.png 248w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071049/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-4-707x1024.png 707w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071049/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-4-166x240.png 166w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071049/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-4-768x1113.png 768w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071049/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-4-1060x1536.png 1060w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1104px) 100vw, 1104px"></p>
<p>Now you don’t even need the couple of dollars.&nbsp;Comicbooks.com has opened an <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read">online reading room</a> stocked with more than 20,000 digitized and restored golden-age comics, with several hundred more issues added every day. Though you may not find Donald Duck there, you will find other “funny animals” — as enthusiasts call them — like <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/funny-animal#s-dizzy-duck-1950-19259"><em>Dizzy Duck</em></a>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/funny-animal#s-lucky-duck-1953"><em>Lucky Duck</em></a>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/funny-animal#s-super-duck-comics-1948"><em>Super Duck Comics</em></a>, and <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/wonder-duck-in-adventure-at-the-rodeo-of-the-fearless-quacker-1"><em>Wonder Duck in Adventure at the Rodeo of the Fearless Quacker</em></a>.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>Those already familiar with the all-American exploits of Archie and his&nbsp;Riverdale High coterie&nbsp;might consider such alternatives as&nbsp;<em><a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/romance#s-candy-1947">Candy</a></em>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/romance#s-tippy-teen-1965"><em>Tippy Teen</em></a>, and <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/humor#s-hap-hazard-1983"><em>Hap Hazard</em></a>. And who needs Superman and Batman when you can enjoy the super-heroic adventures of <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/superhero#s-skyman-1941">Skyman</a>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/superhero#s-bulletman-1941">Bulletman</a>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/superhero#s-cat-man-comics-1941">Cat-Man</a>,&nbsp;and <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/superhero#s-strong-man-1954">Strong Man</a>?</p>
<p><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1127902" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071039/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-3.png" alt width="1110" height="1601" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071039/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-3.png 1110w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071039/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-3-250x360.png 250w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071039/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-3-710x1024.png 710w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071039/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-3-166x240.png 166w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071039/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-3-768x1108.png 768w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2026/08/05071039/OC-Comicbooks-reading-room-3-1065x1536.png 1065w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1110px) 100vw, 1110px"></p>
<p>Given the mainstream prominence of comic books in the mid-twentieth century, these mostly forgotten publications now constitute an especially vivid — at times even lurid — means of pop-cultural time travel, through their content as well as their advertisements (for itching powder and other practical joke supplies, muscle-building and jiu-jitsu courses, war bonds, etc.). In any genre, whether <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/superhero">superhero</a>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/western">western</a>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/romance">romance</a>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/funny-animal">funny animal</a>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/horror">horror</a>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read/genre/science-fiction">science fiction</a>, they offer a connection with readers of decades past. In their form, as you can see on Comicbooks.com’s <a href="https://comicbooks.com/exhibition">timeline of the evolution of what the French call </a><em><a href="https://comicbooks.com/exhibition">le neuvième art</a>, </em><span style="font-size: 13pt;">they offer a connection with even more distant ones: those of&nbsp; </span><a href="https://comicbooks.com/exhibition/pulps">pulp fiction</a>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/exhibition/life">humor weeklies</a>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/exhibition/vanityfair">chromolithograph cartoons</a>, <a href="https://comicbooks.com/exhibition/penny-dreadfuls">penny dreadfuls</a>, and&nbsp;twelfth-century Japanese <a href="https://comicbooks.com/exhibition/choju-giga"><em>chōjū-giga&nbsp;</em>scrolls</a> — the original funny animals. Enter <a href="https://comicbooks.com/read">the free comic collection here</a>.</p>
